Cookie tempering
Cookie can contain plain texts that important message for hackers.
ex)Set-Cookie: logged_in=true; Max-Age=3600; Path=/
Set-Cookie: admin=false; Max-Age=3600; Path=/
In this cookie, attacker can manipulate cookies; ex)curl -H "Cookie: logged_in=true; admin=false" http://10.10.106.21/cookie-test
Answer : logged as a User / if admin=true, attacker can logged in as an admin.

Hashing : cookie values usually become long strings with random characters. and they called hash. but hashes can be easilly manipulated by
[Crackstation.net], the database of billions of hashes and their original strings.

Encoding
It's similar with Hashing in that it creates what would seem a random string of text.
But in fact, the encoding is reversible.
Rerverse the endcoded codes : [https://www.base64decode.org/] *Only if base64.

IDOR
Insecure Direct Object Reference
Occurs when a web server receives user-supplied input to retrieve objects, too much trust has been placed on the input data.
ex) online_service.thm/profile?user_id=1234 , changing user_id=1000 and view another user's info. 
this vulnerability is IDOR.
Not by manipulating a URL, manipulating a query can be IDOR hacking. 
After login, user can post user-id info to server. in dev tool's network tab, it can be detected.
So by manipulating the user-id to another number attacker can see another user's info. (firefox recommended)

